 Rounds with intention They don’t “go play.” They train, test, and refine. So here’s the divide:

Why You Can't Sell E-Mail Marketing to Lululemon Over A Cold Call* How offers break down as they go up market and why Hormozi is spending lots of money to appeal to enterprise clients (following the Tony Robbins' Playbook). You can make the same offer to a smaller business but it wouldn't work on a larger company.  We have a white labeler with a lending client who really struggles to get results.  It didn't make sense to us because we've run multiple successful lending campaigns, and it's very easy to book calls and get leads for them, but this particular client only wanted to service loans around the $50-100 Million dollar range. There are 3 challenges to going up market: 1 They have much better resources and relationships that smaller companies can only dream of, so the offer isn't appealing to them. My stepfather, who didn't even have a very large business, had a dedicated big 4 Bank rep, that could always service liquidity and gave him huge holiday and birthday gifts, Opera tickets, ballet tickets, sixers, eagles tickets whatever he wanted.  And the loans were given at preferential market rates. 2 The need for credibility increases. The larger the company is the more you have to "earn your right" to speak to them.  Some industries value credentials, some value brand, some value track record, but at a certain level you just need to have all three. Alex Hormozi for example has a graet financial track record, but he doesn't have credibility currently to work with Fortune 500 sales teams.  Tony Robbins faced a similar challenge in the 90s and it took a ton of PR and Marketing to get him to work with companies like Nike, Salesforce, and Wells Fargo, but that's a video I'll do later. 3 The appetite for risk decreases A lot of the appeal of smaller vendors, and more compelling offers that have strong guarantees is that when you're small and lets say your email marketing isn't working you really just have less to lose.  But remember that Jaguar ad a while back that did a ton of reputational damage to their brand.  Even if they got a full refund from that marketing agency, it wouldn't matter at all because they lost so much trust and credibility in the market. How to Solve It (Lending Example) For this particular client, the answer is either to go down market, where the offer resonates and gets leads, or to have a stronger more unique and compelling offer that is tailored to larger companies.  We actually had another lending company that would specifically target very large industries like construction and offered them in house financing collateralized against the value of their equipment for situations where they need money but can't get it from a regular bank. That compelling offer was strong enough to book meetings. He could also focus more on existing relationships and referrals if he's not able or willing to do either of those things, but outbound is probably a mistake for him because the offer on it's face doesn't have product market messaging fit.   This situation is actually common with clients that leave a career in Fortune 500, and get their first customers from their rolodex, and then think they can run outbound. It's not as easy as it looks! But when you start to understand how different markets, you can really level up as a marketer. If you guys need help with any of this stuff please reach out, otherwise talk soon *Well, 
